    "result_text": "I've implemented `answer()` in `app/chat.py` and added four tests to `tests/test_chat.py`. All 6 tests pass.\n\n**How it works:**\n- **Retries:** each attempt calls `iter_answer` again, so the whole request starts over. The text is only joined once an attempt completes, so a dropped attempt can't leak fragments into the result. Up to `ATTEMPTS` (3) tries in total.\n- **Timeouts:** these raise `UpstreamTimeout` immediately with no retry. The check comes before the connection-error check because the library treats a timeout as a kind of connection error, so the order matters.\n- **All attempts fail:** raises `UpstreamUnavailable`, with the last error attached as its cause.\n- **Client:** used as given; no dependencies added.\n\n**Logging:** for a network error, whether on connect or mid-stream, the library raises an error whose message is always just `\"Connection error.\"`. The real network error is attached underneath it. So the WARNING on `app.chat` logs that underlying error's type and message. A drop mid-stream logs something like `Attempt 2/3 to reach the model failed: RemoteProtocolError: peer closed connection without sending complete message body`.\n\n**Other points:**\n- Context7 returned \"Monthly quota exceeded\", so I couldn't look up the API there as `CLAUDE.md` asks. I read the installed library source in `.venv` instead to confirm how errors are raised.\n- `history` is copied into a list first, so a history that can only be read once is still sent in full on every retry.\n\n**Tests:** they fake the library's errors in the same way the real library raises them. They cover:\n- a connect failure, then a mid-stream drop, then success: only the complete text is returned, and each warning includes the network error's message\n- every attempt failing: `UpstreamUnavailable`\n- a timeout: `UpstreamTimeout` with only one call made\n- history sent in full on each retry"
  },
